WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.966
Methods of Counting Defect Opportunities

2
00:00:04.966 --> 00:00:07.966
Hello, Mike Harry back with you again.

3
00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:12.432
Where we’re continuing our discussion of defect opportunities.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:17.833
In other words, what must be right or what could go wrong.

5
00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:23.233
Well, you know, those are both from the same coin.

6
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:26.233
Heads or tails. Right or wrong.

7
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:30.766
Either way we look at it, it leads to the same inescapable conclusion.

8
00:00:30.766 --> 00:00:37.232
Now, to bring about our discussion here, of defect opportunities.

9
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:41.233
I want to focus on an industrial and commercial example.

10
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:46.433
Specifically, we’ll start here, with the solder joint right there.

11
00:00:46.433 --> 00:00:53.433
We’re going to be soldering and we’re concerned about soldering opportunities.

12
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:56.566
Defect opportunities where soldering is concerned.

13
00:00:56.566 --> 00:01:02.099
Now, to progress this discussion, let's go over and blow it up.

14
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.600
There again, you can kind of see there’s our solder joint.

15
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.600
And we say to ourselves, well, what could go wrong?

16
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:16.600
Well, in this case. We have several things that could go wrong. Six, to be specific.

17
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.400
It could be missing solder, a joint could miss solder and not having any at all.

18
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.766
It could be insufficient, have pinholes, have voids.

19
00:01:26.766 --> 00:01:30.766
Not be properly wetted to the circuit board.

20
00:01:30.766 --> 00:01:34.266
And the pull strength could be bad. There’s six of them let’s say.

21
00:01:34.266 --> 00:01:39.199
Now, there may be more, may be less, depending upon which

22
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.200
level of inspection that you are applying.

23
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:51.966
For example, there is what we call weapon’s specification for

24
00:01:51.966 --> 00:01:51.999
government business and there's commercial.

25
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000
And of course, the government specifications are more stringent than the commercial.

26
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.700
Therefore, there are more defect categories.

27
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:07.033
Now, the thing we want to really focus on here for a moment, is the idea here.

28
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:13.866
That a defect opportunity, at the opportunistic level,

29
00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:20.799
what we have is that it’s either good or bad. It either passes or fails.

30
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.400
For example, where the solder joint is concerned, and we’re looking at missing solder.

31
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.666
Either the solder is there or it's not. Either it’s sufficient or it's not.

32
00:02:30.666 --> 00:02:37.666
There are pinholes or there's not. It either meets the pull strength criteria or it doesn't.

33
00:02:37.666 --> 00:02:43.299
Now the same thing would hold true with our commercial example.

34
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:50.300
Let's take a transaction. Well with any given transaction, we can say, what must go right?

35
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.366
In other words, what could go wrong? And focus on the defect opportunities

36
00:02:55.366 --> 00:02:58.366
from a transactional prospective.

37
00:02:58.366 --> 00:03:03.366
Well, there would be multiple things that could go wrong with a transaction.

38
00:03:03.366 --> 00:03:10.366
And again, at the opportunity level, it would either be good or it will be bad.

39
00:03:10.366 --> 00:03:16.099
Per opportunity, any given opportunity is either good or it's bad.

40
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:21.100
Now, let's set up a simple example here.

41
00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:27.500
Let's suppose, for the sake of argument, that we have 100 solder joints per circuit board.

42
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:32.766
And there are six different types of defect categories

43
00:03:32.766 --> 00:03:35.899
associated with each solder joint.

44
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:41.900
That's a total of 600 defect opportunities per circuit board.

45
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:48.900
Now, suppose 50 circuit boards are produced. We make 50 of them.

46
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:54.500
Times 600 defect opportunities per board, means

47
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.533
that out of the 50 circuit boards produced,

48
00:03:57.533 --> 00:04:02.433
we will create a total of 3000 defect opportunities.

49
00:04:02.433 --> 00:04:06.433
Well that's pretty simple and straightforward and easy to understand.

50
00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:13.266
Now, let's suppose that we observe 200 defects across the 50 circuit boards.

51
00:04:13.266 --> 00:04:16.832
In other words, there's 200 solder joints that are defective

52
00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:20.833
across the 50 circuit boards that we make.

53
00:04:20.833 --> 00:04:27.866
So, in this case. Here we’ll bring it up, so we can see it here.

54
00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:33.032
200 defects across the 50 boards.

55
00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:39.433
So, the defects per circuit board is computed as 200 over 50, or 4.

56
00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:44.166
That says, we would be averaging, and the keyword there is averaging,

57
00:04:44.166 --> 00:04:48.166
four defects per circuit board.

58
00:04:48.166 --> 00:04:53.366
Now,

59
00:04:53.366 --> 00:05:00.166
hence, the defects per solder joint,

60
00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:04.266
would be four per 100.

61
00:05:04.266 --> 00:05:09.532
Why? There's 100 solder joints per board.

62
00:05:09.533 --> 00:05:16.566
Now, we continue, we can now say.

63
00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:23.099
Scrolling on down here.

64
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.833
We'll enlarge our screen.

65
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:33.133
There we go.

66
00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:39.533
So, the defects per solder joint opportunity would be

67
00:05:39.533 --> 00:05:44.999
.04 divided by 6. Remember, there were

68
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.000
.04 defects per solder joint.

69
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.633
Since there are six unique defect categories, independent

70
00:05:53.633 --> 00:05:58.133
defect categories, per solder joint.

71
00:05:58.133 --> 00:06:02.133
Then we would see .04 over 6 or .0067.

72
00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:06.133
Now we can say, because of that,

73
00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:11.066
the probability of producing a solder joint with zero defects

74
00:06:11.066 --> 00:06:15.199
is e to the minus DPU or 99.33%.

75
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.533
That's to say, the probability of zero defects

76
00:06:20.533 --> 00:06:24.533
in a solder joint is 99.33%.

77
00:06:24.533 --> 00:06:28.533
In other words, were 99.33% certain,

78
00:06:28.533 --> 00:06:34.199
that given the current process capability, we can make a solder joint defect free.

79
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.200
It will pass all six defect categories.

80
00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:43.300
Now owing to that, we can compute the Sigma.

81
00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:50.466
So, the Sigma per solder joint is 3.98.

82
00:06:50.466 --> 00:06:57.399
So, about Four Sigma. So, in this case, we can say that if we produce

83
00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:04.000
this circuit board, make 50 of them, observe 200 defects.

84
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:10.266
Then per defect opportunity, we’re running a capability

85
00:07:10.266 --> 00:07:16.932
of 3.98 Sigma or Four Sigma, about average.

86
00:07:16.933 --> 00:07:23.966
Now, the same exact thing would hold true for the transactional example.

87
00:07:23.966 --> 00:07:30.399
We would have our defect categories and look at the total number of transactions.

88
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.933
So, if we have six defect categories per transaction.

89
00:07:35.933 --> 00:07:39.266
And there are 100 such transactions made,

90
00:07:39.266 --> 00:07:44.032
we would make a total of 600 defect opportunities.

91
00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:51.066
Doesn’t mean they're going to be defective, it just means it's an opportunity for defect.

92
00:07:51.066 --> 00:07:56.066
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
